<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Attarine Madrasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1323">1323</a>–<a href="/years/1325">1325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The al-Attarine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">Madrasa</a> is a college for Islamic studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, next to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarawiyyin-mosque">Qarawiyyin Mosque</a>, built from 1323 to 1325 by the Marinid sultan Abu Said Uthman II. It takes its name from the nearby spice market. Its courtyard is richly decorated with zellij tilework, carved stucco and cedar wood, and it is a masterpiece of Marinid architecture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-attarine-madrasa.jpg" alt="Al-Attarine Madrasa" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Prazeres · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Attarine_Madrasa_DSCF3633_(R_Prazeres).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, an institution that arose in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> by the early 11th century and spread westward, trained scholars above all in Islamic law. It flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> only under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a>, who used their patronage of madrasas to win the loyalty of the independent religious elite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, to present themselves as defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and to train the officials of their state. The al-Attarine, like the nearby Saffarin and Mesbahiyya madrasas, stood beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">Qarawiyyin</a>, the main centre of learning in Fez, and gave lodging and bread to students, many of them poor and from outside the city.</p><p>Construction was supervised by Sheikh Abu Muhammad Abdallah ibn Qasim al-Mizwar, and according to the chronicle Rawd el-Qirtas the sultan watched the laying of the foundations with the local ulema. A habous, a charitable endowment, paid for an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a>, muezzins, teachers and lodging for 50 to 60 students, most of them from towns of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a> and Ksar el-Kebir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> is a two-storey building entered through a bent entrance at the eastern end of Tala'a Kebira street. Galleries line the north and south sides of the courtyard, and the upper floor holds 30 rooms that served as the students' sleeping quarters, a plan close to that of the slightly earlier Madrasa as-Sahrij. The cramped site did not allow the usual alignment of the building with the qibla, so the mihrab stands on the southern wall of the prayer hall, at right angles to the main axis, under a square wooden cupola carved with geometric star patterns.</p><p>The plain exterior contrasts with the interior. Zellij mosaic covers the floor and lower walls, topped by a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>; above it rise carved stucco with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqarnas">muqarnas</a> and arabesques, then carved cedar ending in projecting wooden eaves. The prayer hall has windows of coloured glass set in lead grilles, and the columns carry finely carved capitals. The cedar entrance doors are faced with bronze plating; the originals are in the Dar Batha Museum, and the original bronze chandelier of the prayer hall bears an inscription praising the founder.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Its wood-carving and its metalwork, whose bronze door plating refines the earlier Almoravid plating of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">Qarawiyyin</a>'s doors, are counted among the high points of Marinid art. The building has been classified as a historic monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> since 1915. It has been restored many times in keeping with its original style and is now open to visitors as a historic site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Attarine Madrasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
